      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the number of different orders in which Jordan and his three best friends can run the first three laps, given that Jordan is fixed to run the fourth lap.\n\nSince Jordan is fixed to run the fourth lap, we need to determine the number of permutations of the first three runners for the first three consecutive laps. Essentially, this boils down to finding the number of permutations of the three runners, which is given by \\(3!\\) (3 factorial).\n\nWe can calculate \\(3!\\) as follows:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, each of the three runners has 6 different possibilities to run the first three laps before Jordan. \n\nWe can utilize Python to determine this for confirmation.\n```python\n# The number of permutations for the first three runs (3 runners)\nimport math\n\n# Calculate 3 factorial (3!)\nfirst_runs_permutations = math.factorial(3)\nprint(first_runs_permutations)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe calculation confirms that there are \\(6\\) different ways to arrange the first three runners for the first three laps. Given that Jordan is fixed to run the fourth lap, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run is equal to the number of permutations of the first three runners, which is \\(6\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
