      "text": "$2\\times3\\choose 3 \\times 2$ \n\n$3^{3}$ But these solutions do not account for when Jordan's brother runs a lap when he is already three laps ahead. To find the number of ways the team members can run the race, we need to consider both cases where Jordan's brother runs the fourth lap and where he does not.\n\nCase 1: Jordan's brother runs the fourth lap.\nIn this case, we need to find the number of ways the remaining team members can run the first three laps. There are 3 people and 3 laps, so there are 3! (3 factorial) ways they can run. 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = 6. So, for this case, there are 6 possibilities.\n\nCase 2: Jordan's brother does not run the fourth lap.\nIn this case, Jordan is automatically in the fourth lap after his brother finishes his laps. So, we need to find the number of ways the existing team members can run the first three laps. There are still 3 team members, but now the order they run is fixed (they still have 3 laps). So, there is only 1 way they can run. For the fourth lap, Jordan must run, so we don't need to consider any additional permutations. So, for this case, there is 1 possibility.\n\nTo find the total number of different ways the team members can run the race, we add up both cases. 6 (from case 1) + 1 (from case 2) = 7 possibilities.",
