      "text": "The first place runner can be one of the remaining three runners.\n\nTo determine the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, we can use the concept of permutations for distinct objects taken three at a time. This is known as 4P3, which is equivalent to 4! (4 factorial) divided by 1! (1 factorial).\n\n4P3 = 4! / (4-3)!\n     = 4! / (1!)\n     = (4 × 3 × 2 × 1) / 1\n     = 24\n\nTherefore, the four team members can run in 24 different orders. Of those 24 orders, the first place standing runner could be each of the remaining three.\n\nHence, the answer is: 24",
